"CustomerSummary": "Customer A was an 82-year-old individual with limited investment experience, low annual income (less than $25,000), and modest net worth ($50,000-$99,999). The customer's stated risk tolerance was 'moderate' and investment objective was 'income'. Despite this profile, Lessinger recommended concentrated positions in three successive high-risk closed-end funds that invested primarily in collateralized loan obligation vehicles (CLOs) and below investment grade securities that carried significant risk of loss. These investments represented between 23% and 37% of the customer's net worth at different times. The Care Obligation violation occurred because Lessinger failed to make recommendations in the customer's best interest, instead recommending investments that were inconsistent with the customer's conservative investment profile. The concentrated positions in speculative, high-risk investments were inappropriate given the customer's age, limited finances, and moderate risk tolerance. As a result, Customer A lost $5,029.85.",

"OverallSummary": "This case involves a Care Obligation violation under Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) by Victor Alan Lessinger, who recommended unsuitable high-risk investments to an elderly customer with limited financial resources. The primary violation was recommending that an 82-year-old customer with limited investment experience, low income, and moderate risk tolerance invest a significant percentage of her net worth (ranging from 23% to 37% at different times) in high-risk closed-end funds that invested in below-investment grade securities and CLOs. These funds were explicitly described in their prospectuses as carrying heightened risk of substantial loss. The analytical steps that established this Care Obligation violation included: (1) examination of the customer's investment profile (age, income, net worth, investment experience, risk tolerance); (2) assessment of the risk characteristics of the recommended investments; (3) evaluation of the concentration levels of these high-risk investments relative to the customer's net worth; and (4) determination that these recommendations created a risk of loss inconsistent with the customer's investment profile. As a result of these unsuitable recommendations, the customer suffered a loss of $5,029.85. Lessinger was sanctioned with a three-month suspension, a $5,000 fine, and ordered to pay restitution of $5,029.85 plus interest to the customer."
